ages up to two  languages that are similar let's suppose  that as an American  you want to learn Italian Spanish and  French at the same time I also remember  vividly remember conversation with  Richard Richard is a fellow polygon who  told me that the University when he when  he started learning for example  Portuguese Spanish and in French if I if  memory serves me if I remember correctly  he said in most students were actually  very confused because they were trying  to tackle very similar languages  languages tend to overlap for a number  of reasons for example Italian and  Spanish 2/10 overlap for pronunciation  because it's in a way it's very similar  and anole morphology as well like very  many words are pretty similar so I would  say first I suggest you learn just one  language at a time because as I said  before imagine if you have ten years if  you learn for example two languages at  the same time for two years and then you  move on to other languages then you  develop a certain capacity in your choir  and ability form a language score in a  certain language that is going to help  you learn another language when two  languages are similar if you for example  decide to learn a German and Spanish at  the beginning and after a couple years  to try to tackle a time it will be much  easier maybe at the beginning you're  going to confuse them a little bit but  the fact that you have acquired a  language core so to say so a structure a  layer in one language it gives you a lot  of help when it comes to building  another language core which is in a way  similar while if you try to build two  language cores that are similar at the  same time they're going to overlap so as  a general guideline I would say first if  you really want to learn multiple  languages at the same time I would say  that two is the amount the max right huh  so try to maybe two languages so choose  a maximum of two languages at any given  time then choose two languages that are  possibly distinct from one another so  they're different because as I said  before if they're similar they can  overlap in by way words grammar emotions  memories etc etc I try to use another  another thing useful piece of advice is  try to choose an easy language and a  relatively  got one now easy and difficult you know  it would take a century to explain the  definition of that but I would say that  by easy language I would say that it's a  language that is similar in so many ways  to yours that an example is England for  example English and Dutch in Dutch is  considered one of the easiest language  for native English speakers for a number  of reasons or a time Spanish and Italian  for example or in Spanish and Portuguese  and and another thing is once you've  decided to you have an easy language in  a difficult one you can organize your  time accordingly and better because for  example you can dedicate 70 to 80  percent of your time to the difficult  language and 20-30 percent of your time  to an easy an easy language that  requires less effort you know and I did  that for example when I started learning  Portuguese and Chinese I dedicated 80  percent of my time to Chinese but I also  allocated some time to Portuguese at the  end of my Chinese session I started  learning a little bit of Portuguese and  that was very helpful because just  fifteen to thirty minutes a day of  Portuguese help me you know in six  months I was already fluent because  Portuguese is very very similar to  Spanish a language that I already learn  if I learn Portuguese and Spanish at the  same time it would have been a disaster  for my brain and the last thing that I  wanted to add is that when you learn two  languages at the same time remember to  study both of them every day so don't do  any sort of rotation meaning you study a  language for a week and then you study  another language  the next week because as I said before  you have to give your brain the  possibility of growing and by doing if  you want to do if you want that to  happen you have to give it every day  some pieces of information you know if  you for example you study a language for  one week and then you study another one  what about the first language during the  second week you're not going to do  anything so it Ted you did the amount of  information that you that you accumulate  in your brain is goes down and then you  have to take it back again so right  organizing your time efficiently it's  very important and try to study every  single day both languages the more  languages you learn obviously the more  time you need the more time and energy  you need so just as  what kind of investment you want to make  to make and if you have maybe 10 hours a  day to study languages then you can you  can possibly tackle 3 for language  nobody nobody can keep you from doing  that but remember that the more  languages you learn the more difficult  it gets to organize your time and not to  mix languages
 

